Lab 3: Managing Glance (Image Service)
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Upload and manage VM images using OpenStack Glance.
Configure Glance for efficient image retrieval.
Use cloud-init for automated first-boot configuration during instance launch.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack environment with Glance service installed.
Administrative access to the OpenStack CLI (openstack command).
A base cloud image (e.g., CirrOS, Ubuntu Cloud, or CentOS Cloud).
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and YAML syntax.
Task 1: Upload Base Images to Glance
Subtask 1.1: Download a Base Cloud Image
Download a lightweight cloud image (e.g., CirrOS) for testing:
wget https://download.cirros-cloud.net/0.6.1/cirros-0.6.1-x86_64-disk.img
Expected Outcome: The image file (cirros-0.6.1-x86_64-disk.img) is downloaded.
Subtask 1.2: Upload the Image to Glance
Use the OpenStack CLI to upload the image:

openstack image create "cirros-0.6.1" \
  --file cirros-0.6.1-x86_64-disk.img \
  --disk-format qcow2 \
  --container-format bare \
  --public
--disk-format qcow2: Specifies the disk format.
--container-format bare: Indicates no container format.
--public: Makes the image available to all projects.
Verify the upload:

openstack image list
Expected Outcome: The image appears in the list with status active.

Troubleshooting Tip: If the upload fails, check disk space in Glance's storage backend (/var/lib/glance/images/).

Task 2: Configure Glance for Image Management
Subtask 2.1: Set Image Properties
Add metadata to the image for better management:

openstack image set \
  --property os_distro=cirros \
  --property os_version=0.6.1 \
  cirros-0.6.1
Verify properties:

openstack image show cirros-0.6.1
Expected Outcome: The output includes os_distro and os_version properties.

Subtask 2.2: Enable Image Caching (Optional)
Edit Glance configuration to enable caching (if supported):
sudo vi /etc/glance/glance-api.conf
Add/modify:
[glance_store]
default_store = file
filesystem_store_datadir = /var/lib/glance/images/
Restart the service:
sudo systemctl restart openstack-glance-api
Task 3: Use cloud-init for First-Boot Configuration
Subtask 3.1: Create a cloud-init Configuration File
Create a cloud-init.yaml file to set a password and SSH key:
#cloud-config
password: openshift123
chpasswd: { expire: False }
ssh_pwauth: True
ssh_authorized_keys:
  - ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2E... user@example.com
Subtask 3.2: Launch an Instance with cloud-init
Create an instance using the uploaded image and cloud-init:

openstack server create \
  --image cirros-0.6.1 \
  --flavor m1.small \
  --nic net-id=<NETWORK_ID> \
  --user-data cloud-init.yaml \
  cirros-instance
Replace <NETWORK_ID> with your network’s ID.

Verify the instance boots with the configured password/key:

openstack console log show cirros-instance | grep -i "cloud-init"
Expected Outcome: Logs show successful cloud-init execution.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Uploaded a base image to Glance and configured its properties.
Explored Glance’s image management capabilities.
Automated instance configuration using cloud-init.
Next Steps: Experiment with larger images (e.g., Ubuntu/CentOS) and advanced cloud-init modules (e.g., package installation, disk setup).

Troubleshooting Appendix
Image upload fails: Check Glance service logs (journalctl -u openstack-glance-api).
cloud-init not working: Ensure the image supports cloud-init (most cloud images do).
Missing network: Verify the network ID with openstack network list.
